 "In Dogon religion of Mali, the Nommo are ancestral spirits associated with water, often described as amphibious or fish-like beings who brought knowledge and order. Marcel Griaule\'s mid-20th-century ethnography made them famous, intertwining them with creation narratives and—controversially—with astronomical lore about Sirius. Later anthropologists questioned how much of Griaule\'s systematic cosmology reflected prompting versus independent Dogon teaching.",
