X('orontius-finaeus','The Orontius Finaeus (Oronce Fine) map of 1531',H,'disputed',3,1531,['cartographic','textual'],
 "French cartographer Oronce Fine (Orontius Finaeus) published a 1531 world map whose southern landmass some 20th-century writers, notably Charles Hapgood, compared to an ice-free Antarctica. Hapgood argued such maps preserved knowledge from an unknown Ice Age civilisation. Mainstream historians of cartography treat Fine\'s south land as a speculative Terra Australis of Renaissance geography, not a survey of Antarctica under ice.",
 [('Hapgood / lost civilisation','Alternative historians still cite Fine and the Piri Reis map as evidence of prehistoric Antarctic mapping; professional cartographic history rejects that reading.'),
  ('Authentic Antarctica','No peer-reviewed geophysical match requires an ice-free surveyed Antarctica in Fine\'s sources.')],
 ['wiki:Oronce_Fine','wiki:Charles_Hapgood'],img='Oronce_Fine'),
X('archimedes','Archimedes of Syracuse',H,'confirmed',1,-287,['textual'],
 "Archimedes (c. 287–212 BCE) of Syracuse was a Greek mathematician and engineer who proved results on areas, volumes and the lever, approximated pi, and invented war machines used against Rome in the siege of Syracuse. Later sources credit a heat-ray of burning mirrors and a claw that lifted ships; modern reconstructions of the mirrors are generally unsuccessful at warship scale, while crane-like \"claws\" are more plausible. He was killed when Syracuse fell.",
 [('Burning mirrors','The heat-ray story is late and physically dubious at fleet scale; it remains a popular experimental challenge.'),
  ('Antikythera link','Some popular accounts loosely credit Archimedes with the Antikythera mechanism\'s tradition; he worked in the right cultural sphere, but authorship is unproven.')],
 ['wiki:Archimedes'],img='Archimedes'),
X('ibn-al-haytham','Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen) and the scientific method in optics',H,'confirmed',1,1021,['textual'],
 "Hasan Ibn al-Haytham (c. 965–1040), working in Cairo, wrote the Book of Optics (Kitab al-Manazir), arguing that vision results from light entering the eye, analysing reflection and refraction, and stressing controlled testing of claims. Latin translations as Alhazen influenced medieval and early modern European optics. He also wrote on astronomy, mathematics and the camera obscura.",
 [('Father of scientific method','Popular histories sometimes call him the sole inventor of experimental science; historians place him among several medieval figures developing empirical argument.'),
  ('Suppressed genius myths','Internet essays claim the Church uniquely suppressed him; his work was in fact transmitted into Latin Christendom.')],
 ['wiki:Ibn_al-Haytham'],img='Ibn_al-Haytham'),
X('cairo-geniza','The Cairo Geniza',H,'confirmed',2,1896,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Cairo Geniza is a vast cache of Jewish manuscript fragments (roughly 9th–19th centuries) recovered mainly from the Ben Ezra Synagogue in Fustat (Old Cairo), brought to scholarly attention especially by Solomon Schechter in the 1890s. Letters, contracts, liturgies and shopping lists illuminate Mediterranean social, economic and religious life. Most fragments are now in Cambridge and other libraries; digitisation continues.",
 [('Hidden Atlantis of papers','Romantic accounts exaggerate secret cabals; the geniza was a sacred storeroom for worn writings, not a sealed conspiracy archive.'),
  ('Lost gospel fantasies','Occasional media claims of world-changing suppressed gospels from the geniza usually misfile ordinary liturgical or legal texts.')],
 ['wiki:Cairo_Geniza'],img='Cairo_Geniza',changed='Mass scholarly exploitation from the 1890s; major digitisation in the 21st century.'),
X('grauballe-man','Grauballe Man and the bog bodies of northern Europe',H,'confirmed',2,-290,['archaeological','forensic'],
 "Grauballe Man is a bog body found in 1952 in Jutland, Denmark, dated to around the 3rd century BCE. His throat was cut, and the acidic peat preserved soft tissue to a rare degree. Together with Tollund Man and other finds he anchors debate over Iron Age ritual killing, execution and deposition in wetlands across northern Europe.",
 [('Sacrifice vs execution','Scholars disagree whether bog bodies were votive sacrifices, punished criminals, or both depending on case.'),
  ('Alien autopsy vibes','Sensational media sometimes exoticises bog bodies as inexplicable anomalies; forensic archaeology explains preservation chemically.')],
 ['wiki:Grauballe_Man','wiki:Bog_body'],img='Grauballe_Man'),
X('denisova-cave','Denisova Cave and the discovery of Denisovans',H,'confirmed',1,-200000,['genetic','archaeological','radiometric'],
 "Denisova Cave in the Altai Mountains of Siberia yielded a finger bone whose DNA, sequenced in 2010, revealed a previously unknown archaic human group—the Denisovans—distinct from Neanderthals and modern humans. Later finds include a jaw in Tibet and genetic traces in present-day Melanesians and others. The cave was also used by Neanderthals and modern humans, making it a key contact zone.",
 [('Species status','Whether Denisovans are a separate species or a subspecies remains taxonomically open; they are a clear genetic lineage.'),
  ('Hidden advanced race','Fringe readings cast Denisovans as a lost technological civilisation; the archaeological footprint so far is sparse stone and bone, not cities.')],
 ['wiki:Denisova_Cave','wiki:Denisovan','doi:10.1038/nature08976'],img='Denisova_Cave',changed='2010 DNA paper established a new archaic human group from minimal fossil remains.'),
